A||N||7||Jaygee79||How to remove luggage rack||||5||
Z||000000||Jaygee79||08-30-2000||03:14 PM||||The luggage rack on my 84 SE needs painting badly.  Is there any way to take it off?  There are some screws underneath, but there has to be more holding it on than that.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-30-2000||03:27 PM||||Mine looks like it was put on before the lid was fully assembled. I don't think it will come off without a big mess. I think you'll need to figure out a good way to mask it and refinish it in place.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jaygee79||08-30-2000||03:30 PM||||That's what I was afraid of.  Thanks for the info.||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||08-30-2000||03:42 PM||||You'll have to pry the rubber strips that run across the top edges up from a corner and peel them off.  They will slide back into the grooves when your ready to replace them.  I think there's about 12 bolts/screws underneath those strips holding the racks to the deck.  I'm not for certain if those just screw into the rubber seal or if there's a nut on the backside.  I don't think there's is, but I'm not for sure without checking mine...  maybe someone knows.<P>I know for sure you can remove just the rubber strips and mask off the car, scuff and re-paint them.  Then armor all the rubber strips real good and replace them when the rack is dry.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-30-2000||03:49 PM||||The few screws/studs I can see have nuts on them. I don't know about the early years but my 87 has those nasty cone nuts with gaskets on them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||08-30-2000||04:06 PM||||The screws you see when you pry out the rubber strips go into rubber expansion nuts that are inserted into the decklid from the outside.  Removing the rack is really fairly easy.  Pull out the rubber strips, remove the screws on the top and the nuts on the bottom side of the decklid and lift off the pieces.  If nescessary, the rubber anchor nuts can be carefully pried out from the top with a small screwdriver.  I did my 84 a couple of years ago and had no problems.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||08-30-2000||04:13 PM||||Yep, kool. you should be all set to take it off and scuff everything and paint it to look like new!||||reg||14
Z||000007||Jaygee79||08-30-2000||05:07 PM||||Thanks everyone for the info.  I'll let you know how it turns out.||||reg||1
